      "content": "So you're saying the location essentially acts as a shortcut for the model to make assumptions? If we were to look at the actual data driving those \"patterns,\" would we find that these urban areas are just higher risk, or is it more about the demographic makeup of those neighborhoods? Like, is the risk actually coming from the people living there or just the zip code itself?",
